// Pull request author: Fz0x00
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential Malicious Extension Loading via Chromium Flag on Linux
// Sigma ID: 888fb8a4-5004-4449-b3b2-a42da785f21f
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: PersonalRules
// Date: 2026-04-21 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: persistence, T1176.001
// Description: Detects the use of the '--load-extension' Chromium flag on any process on Linux. This parameter
//   exists in all Chromium-based applications including browsers (Chrome, Edge, Brave, etc.),
//   Electron apps (Discord, Slack, VS Code, Teams, etc.), and CEF framework applications.
//   Adversaries may abuse this feature to load malicious extensions for persistence, credential
//   theft, cookie stealing, or browser session hijacking.
// False positives:
//   - Automated testing frameworks such as Selenium, Cypress, or Playwright
//   - Browser extension development and debugging
//   - Electron app development using remote debugging or custom extensions
// References:
//   - https://redcanary.com/blog/chromeloader/
//   - https://github.com/cedowens/Dump-Chrome-Cookies
//   - https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/api/command-line-switches
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/bd297b1f47c28f115f976fb5272ebff536607a19/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_browsers_chromium_load_extension.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "--load-extension="
